codex
**6.5/10 combined.** Long Cat Out! has one genuinely fun move: send the right cat, finish a carrier, and watch three more cats jump off the bench. The cats have personality, and the finished rooftop earns the café its screen time. But too much of the puzzle is clearing whichever cat can move until there’s a bench-space test. The café runs out of builds around L60, and the best chain is easier to watch than to challenge a friend to reproduce. I reran the core and economy checks. This score is a design judgment from the frames and code, without player retention or sharing data.

1. **Make the generator reward planning a chain.** All 600 levels pass the checks. On the intended paths through L5–14, 21 of 103 turns had a single legal exit, and on 65 every legal exit still left a possible win. Fourteen of the 17 losing alternatives overflowed the bench immediately. Teach players to set something up. Require at least one fork on a normal board where both exits are survivable, but one frees a key lane or sets up a three-cat hop within the next two taps. Score those forks across several viable paths. Right now the band scores traps and chains along one scripted path. [Solver](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/search.js:305>) · [Generator](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/generate.js:766>)

2. **Show exactly where the cat will go before the move commits.** The L5 frame asks players to connect three pink cats on cushions with a pink carrier two slots away, while another badge floats at the end of the belt. Give the queue fixed slots labeled **Now**, **Next**, and **Later**, with fill pips on the active carrier. On touch-down, light the actual destination cushions or carrier slots for both mother and kitten, in arrival order. Commit on release and let players drag off to cancel. Keep a mystery cat’s color hidden in the preview. [Input handling](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/game/game.js:357>)

3. **Rebuild L3 around a mistake the player can recover from.** In the frame, the pink pair has already taken two of three seats, and the lesson still says “Kittens follow mom out!” The player made the fatal choice before the game explained the cost. Let pink-first survive with a cramped bench and let orange-first trigger the satisfying chain. Update the lesson the instant the pair parks. Put the first irreversible version of that trap after the player has seen the tradeoff. [L3 layout](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/generate.js:166>)

4. **Follow each mechanic lesson with a board where timing it changes the solve.** Twelve of the 25 boards from L15–39 contain none of the dog, yarn, mystery, or ice mechanics. The generator checks whether a mechanic holds up a cat on the scripted route. Give it a stronger job. For yarn, author a board where rolling the ball early opens a matching exit and saves a seat, while rolling it late forces a detour. Give every mechanic a teach, practice, and remix sequence before leaving its appearances to chance. [Mechanic filter](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/generate.js:707>)

5. **Turn the L13 chain into a playable dare.** The giant stamp gets the best real estate, and the win card shrinks the action beside a $4.99 offer. Make a full-width, four-second loop that starts on the setup tap and keeps the cushions and carrier in view through the third hop. End on “Can you chain all 3?” with a link that opens that exact board. Drop “Dodged a trap” until it comes from the player’s moves. Today it reads from generator metadata. [Win and share logic](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/main.js:324>)

6. **Give the finished café a next goal.** The economy test finishes all five areas and unlocks the eighth resident by L60. The rooftop frame shows the consequence at L61: “Area complete!” and a huge Play button. Give Captain a recurring rooftop project with three visible upgrades and a decor choice the player can place. Preview the first project before the final build, so players know what the next stars buy. [Café pacing test](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/scripts/check-econ.mjs:235>)

7. **Make a loss teach the next attempt.** The L49 sheet preserves the stalled cat, but three rescue tiles dominate the screen, including a 120-coin seat when the player has 50. Use a short bottom sheet, keep the board visible, and highlight the tap that spent the last seat. Give players one free **Take Back** on their first bench failure, with **Retry** primary thereafter. Put the unaffordable seat behind the Shop and keep the Starter Bundle off the chain celebration. [Continue sheet](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/ui/ui.js:267>)

8. **Make Cat Show a weekly puzzle worth comparing.** Ship one authored board per week with a fixed bench, unlimited practice, and a result friends can compare. Start with lowest peak bench occupancy, with biggest chain as the tiebreaker. Award a decoration players can actually place for beating the target. Today the headline prize comes from a 44-ribbon quota, and the earned decor is stored without being rendered in the café. [Cat Show rewards](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/game/econ.js:132>)
